PROJECT STRUCTURE COMPARISON
============================

Compared documents:
- PROJECT_STRUCTURE.dat: Transient_Diffusion2D_FDM_Solvers_Cpp
- Another_PROJECT_STRUCTURE.dat: Transient_Diffusion1D_FDM_Solvers_Cpp

SCOPE AND EVIDENCE
------------------
This report is derived only from the two architecture-map files named above. It
compares documented paths, responsibilities, numerical model descriptions, and
CMake target descriptions. Source and header contents were not inspected, so
"unchanged" means structurally equivalent and documented with the same role; it
does not prove byte-for-byte source identity. Any shared code should therefore be
validated separately before extracting it into a GitHub library.

HIGH-LEVEL RELATIONSHIP
-----------------------
Both projects are C++20 finite-difference solvers for transient diffusion and use
the same broad layered architecture:

  Config -> Core -> Discretization -> LinearSolvers
          -> SimulationSetup -> IO/Utilities/Verification -> main executables

The current project is the two-dimensional evolution of the other documented
architecture. It preserves most domain-independent infrastructure, changes the
field/mesh model from one dimension to two dimensions, adds an ADI subsystem, and
adds contour visualization. The other project is explicitly one-dimensional and
uses a flatter, older matrix/plot directory layout in its documented tree.

MODEL-LEVEL DIFFERENCES
-----------------------

Current project: two-dimensional transient diffusion
- Rectangular domain with Width and Height.
- Uniform Nx by Ny mesh and a row-major Field[j][i].
- Boundary values on Top, Bottom, Right, and Left walls.
- Directional X/Y ADI sweeps and one-dimensional line fields.
- Contour, line, and live visualization.

Other project: one-dimensional transient diffusion
- One-dimensional domain length.
- N-node mesh and one-dimensional field/profile.
- Documented active setup uses top/bottom Dirichlet values for its 1D model.
- No ADI directory or X/Y sweep subsystem is documented.
- Live and saved line plotting are documented; contour plotting is not.

STRUCTURALLY COMMON FILES AND MODULES
-------------------------------------
The following components appear in both maps with the same main responsibility.
They are the strongest candidates for common GitHub libraries. Their documented
source contents were not compared, so keep their APIs stable only after a source
level review.

1. Build and configuration foundations
- CMakeLists.txt: CMake project configuration, target definitions, dependencies,
  compiler settings, and executable output configuration.
- .clangd: clangd configuration.
- .gitignore: version-control ignore rules.
- .vscode/launch.json and .vscode/tasks.json: editor launch/build task support.

2. Core boundary metadata
- Core/Boundary/Boundary.hpp and Boundary.cpp: boundary values and boundary object
  construction.
- Core/Boundary/BoundaryManager/BoundaryManager.hpp: boundary type, orientation,
  and location enums.

3. Core scalar-field infrastructure
- Core/Field/Field.hpp and Field.cpp: discrete field storage and field operations.
- Core/Field/FieldName/FieldName.hpp: identifiers used for output file naming.

The responsibility is shared, but the storage contract differs: the current map
documents a 2D row-major field and row/column operations, while the other map
describes a 1D nodal field. A future common library should separate a generic field
interface from dimensional storage.

4. Domain and mesh abstractions
- Core/Geometry/Geometry.hpp and Geometry.cpp.
- Core/Mesh/Mesh.hpp and Mesh.cpp.

The class roles are common, but the geometry and mesh contracts differ. The current
project has rectangular Width/Height and Nx/Ny; the other has Length and N nodes.
The implementation should therefore be shared only through dimension-independent
validation and coordinate utilities, or through separate 1D/2D adapters.

5. Scheme identification
- Discretization/SolverScheme/SolverScheme.hpp.

Both maps document FTCS, DuFort-Frankel, Laasonen, and Crank-Nicolson identifiers.
The enum/name conversion is a strong common-library candidate if the public values
and spelling remain compatible.

6. Explicit discretization schemes
- Discretization/Explicit/FTCS/FTCS.hpp and FTCS.cpp.
- Discretization/Explicit/DuFortFrankel/DuFortFrankel.hpp and DuFortFrankel.cpp.
- Discretization/Explicit/FTCS/FTCS_Stability.hpp.

Both architectures document the same explicit scheme modules. The numerical kernels
may still differ because a 1D stencil and a 2D stencil have different neighbors and
diffusion numbers. The stability helper is a particularly good common candidate only
if it is factored around a generic diffusion-number policy.

7. Non-ADI implicit discretization modules
- Discretization/Implicit/Boundary/EnforceBoundary.hpp and EnforceBoundary.cpp.
- Discretization/Implicit/CrankNicolson/CrankNicolsonAssembler.hpp and .cpp.
- Discretization/Implicit/Laasonen/LaasonenAssembler.hpp and .cpp.

Both maps contain these module families. Their matrix assembly is likely dimension
dependent, so the shared part is the scheme contract and scalar coefficient logic;
the stencil and boundary assembly may need dimension-specific implementations.

8. Linear solver algorithms
- LinearSolvers/GaussianElimination/GaussianElimination.hpp and .cpp.
- LinearSolvers/ThomasAlgorithm/ThomasAlgorithm.hpp and .cpp.

The solver algorithms are documented with the same roles in both projects and are
the best candidates for standalone, dimension-independent libraries. They operate
on matrix/vector abstractions, so matrix storage API compatibility must be checked.

9. Output naming and file writing
- IO/OutputWriter/FileWriter/FileWriter.hpp and FileWriter.cpp.
- IO/OutputWriter/FileName/FileName.hpp.
- IO/OutputWriter/Label/Label.hpp.
- IO/OutputWriter/Legend/Legend.hpp.
- IO/PathManager/PathManager/PathManager.hpp.
- IO/PathManager/PathType/PathType.hpp.

Both maps document the same output responsibilities and naming/path concepts. The
field serialization format may differ because the current project writes 2D field
nodes while the other project is 1D. Path and naming helpers are strong candidates
for a common IO support library; field writers should expose a dimension-neutral
record iterator or separate 1D/2D adapters.

10. Utilities
- Utilities/Convergence/RelativeFieldChange/FieldChange.hpp and FieldChange.cpp.
- Utilities/Convergence/RelativeFieldChange/TimeScale/TimeScale.hpp.
- Utilities/KeyboardHandler/KeyboardHandler.hpp.
- Utilities/Parameter/Parameter.hpp.
- Utilities/StatusPrinter/StatusPrinter.hpp.

The responsibility and documented paths are common. These are strong candidates for
a shared utilities library, subject to checking naming and field-size assumptions.

11. Analytical verification
- Verification/AnalyticalSolution/AnalyticalSolution.hpp and .cpp.

Both maps contain an analytical-solution module, but the mathematical solution is
dimension and boundary-condition specific. Share only the verification interface,
series helpers, or comparison utilities; keep the 1D and 2D solution kernels in
separate packages.

PATHS OR ORGANIZATION THAT DIFFER
---------------------------------

1. LinearSolvers matrix storage
Other project documents:
- LinearSolvers/Matrix/CoefficientMatrix/
- LinearSolvers/Matrix/RHS/
- LinearSolvers/Matrix/TridiagonalMatrix/

Current project documents:
- LinearSolvers/Matrix/GaussianElimination_Matrix/CoefficientMatrix/
- LinearSolvers/Matrix/GaussianElimination_Matrix/RHS/
- LinearSolvers/Matrix/ADI_Matrix/RHS/
- LinearSolvers/Matrix/ADI_Matrix/TridiagonalMatrix/

The current project separates dense Gaussian-elimination storage from ADI storage.
This is an architectural refinement rather than a direct path-compatible layout.
The reusable library boundary should be the matrix/vector interfaces and algorithms,
with storage backends grouped by solver family.

2. Plotting directories
Other project documents:
- IO/Visualization/gnuplot/LivePointPlotter/
- IO/Visualization/gnuplot/LinePlot/

Current project documents:
- IO/Visualization/gnuplot/LivePointPlotter/
- IO/Visualization/gnuplot/LinePlot/
- IO/Visualization/gnuplot/ContourPlot/

LivePointPlotter and LinePlot are structurally common. ContourPlot is current-project
specific because it consumes 2D field data.

3. Plot dashboard location
Other project documents a plot dashboard under:
- SimulationSetup/PostProcessing/Plot/Plot.hpp and Plot.cpp.

Current project documents it under:
- SimulationSetup/PostProcessing/PlotDashboard/Plot/Plot.hpp and Plot.cpp.

The dashboard role is common, but the path and current composition differ. The
current dashboard aggregates live, line, and contour plotters.

4. Simulation setup
Both maps contain SimulationParams, SimulationBoundaries, and SimulationContext.
The current project additionally documents:
- SimulationSetup/ADI_Method/ADI.hpp and ADI.cpp.

The ADI state owner has no counterpart in the other project.

5. Discretization organization
The other map groups Laasonen and Crank-Nicolson directly under
Discretization/Implicit/. The current project keeps those non-ADI assemblers there
and adds separate directional ADI assemblers under ADI/Discretization/.

6. Result layout
Both maps document Initial_Field, Analytical_Field, and Numerical_Field outputs.
The current map documents scheme-specific numerical directories for FTCS,
DUFORT_FRANKEL, Laasonen, and CrankNicolson and is designed for 2D snapshots and
contours. The other map documents the same scheme grouping without 2D/contour detail.

CURRENT-PROJECT-ONLY COMPONENTS
-------------------------------
- ADI/SweepDirection/SweepDirection.hpp.
- ADI/Field1D/Field1D.hpp and Field1D.cpp.
- ADI/Discretization/Boundary/XSweepBoundary/ and YSweepBoundary/.
- ADI/Discretization/Laasonen/XSweepAssembler/ and YSweepAssembler/.
- ADI/Discretization/CrankNicolson/XSweepAssembler/ and YSweepAssembler/.
- SimulationSetup/ADI_Method/ADI.hpp and ADI.cpp.
- IO/Visualization/gnuplot/ContourPlot/ContourPlot.hpp and ContourPlot.cpp.
- 2D wall configuration and row/column field operations.
- cfd_adi and the cfd_all_libraries aggregate target.

OTHER-PROJECT-ONLY OR NOT DOCUMENTED IN CURRENT MAP
----------------------------------------------------
- The other project describes a one-dimensional Geometry/Length and Mesh/N contract.
- The other project documents a flat Matrix/CoefficientMatrix, Matrix/RHS, and
  Matrix/TridiagonalMatrix layout that is not the current layout.
- The other project does not document an ADI subsystem or ContourPlot module.
- The other project documents its plot dashboard at SimulationSetup/PostProcessing/Plot/.
- The current project does not document those old flat matrix paths or that old
  dashboard path.

LIBRARY EXTRACTION RECOMMENDATION
---------------------------------
The following shared libraries can be planned with the lowest coupling:

1. cfd_common_utilities
   Parameter, status, keyboard, time-scale, and relative-change utilities.

2. cfd_linear_algebra
   ThomasAlgorithm, GaussianElimination, RHS abstractions, and matrix interfaces.
   Keep dense and ADI storage adapters separate where their dimensions differ.

3. cfd_io_support
   FileName, Label, Legend, PathManager, PathType, and generic field record writing.
   Put 1D/2D serialization adapters behind a common writer contract.

4. cfd_scheme_contracts
   SolverScheme identifiers, generic time-step interfaces, and shared stability/
   coefficient helpers. Keep 1D/2D stencil implementations in separate modules.

5. cfd_core_geometry_boundary
   Boundary metadata, enum types, geometry validation, and coordinate utilities.
   Provide separate 1D and 2D geometry/mesh implementations behind common concepts.

6. cfd_plotting_core
   Shared gnuplot process/data handling, LivePointPlotter, LinePlot, and dashboard
   interfaces. Keep ContourPlot in a 2D visualization extension.

Components that should remain project-specific:
- 1D and 2D analytical solution kernels.
- 1D versus 2D Field and Mesh storage contracts.
- ADI sweep assemblers, ADI boundary enforcement, and ADI state management.
- Scheme-specific 1D/2D matrix assembly where stencil dimensions differ.

COMPATIBILITY RISKS BEFORE SHARING CODE
---------------------------------------
- Identical filenames do not establish identical APIs or data layouts.
- The current Field uses [j][i] row-major indexing; the other project is 1D.
- Current matrix paths are split into ADI_Matrix and GaussianElimination_Matrix.
- Current plotting includes 2D contours; the other map only documents line plotting.
- Configuration names differ: current Width/Height, alpha, Nx/Ny; other Length, nu,
  and N. A shared library should consume parameter concepts instead of project-specific
  SolverInputs names.
- CMake target names and source lists differ. Extracted libraries should expose stable
  targets and public include directories rather than copying one project's paths.
- Boundary condition enum support may exceed the conditions actually implemented by
  a solver path; preserve that distinction in a shared API.

SUMMARY FOR THE OTHER AI MODEL
------------------------------
The two projects share the same high-level CFD solver architecture and many supporting
modules: boundary metadata, field/mesh/geometry concepts, four named time schemes,
Gaussian and Thomas solvers, output naming/writing, utilities, plotting foundations,
simulation context, and analytical verification interfaces. The current project is a
2D extension with rectangular mesh semantics, four wall boundaries, ADI line solves,
ADI-specific matrix storage, directional assemblers, and contour plotting. The safest
first shared GitHub libraries are utilities, linear solvers, output/path helpers, and
plotting process infrastructure. Core field/mesh classes, analytical kernels, and ADI
assemblers should remain dimension-specific until their interfaces are deliberately
generalized.
